artifact.self_contained_problem:
A decentralized partially observable Markov decision process (Dec-POMDP) is a 7-tuple ⟨S, {A_i}_{i=1}^N, T, R, {Ω_i}_{i=1}^N, O, γ⟩ where S is a set of states, A_i is the action set of agent i (with joint action set A = ∏_i A_i), T(s'|s,a) is a conditional transition kernel, R: S × A → ℝ is a reward function, Ω_i is the observation set of agent i (with joint observation set Ω = ∏_i Ω_i), O(o|s',a) is a conditional observation kernel, and γ ∈ [0,1] is a discount factor.

A multi-agent causal model (MACM) for N agents is a collection of structural causal models {M_i}_{i=1}^N, one per agent, together with a designated set of shared endogenous variables that appear in more than one of the M_i. Each M_i consists of a directed acyclic graph over endogenous and exogenous variables and a system of structural assignments determining each endogenous variable from its parents. The shared variables are constrained to have identical structural assignments across all agents in which they appear, so that interventions and counterfactual queries on shared variables are well-defined across the multi-agent system. An MACM supports Pearl's three rungs of causal inference: associational (L1), interventional (L2), and counterfactual (L3) queries.

Problem. Provide a construction that reframes an arbitrary Dec-POMDP as a multi-agent causal model with explicit shared variables, specifying:
(i) the variables (states S_t, joint and per-agent actions A_t, A_{i,t}, joint and per-agent observations O_t, O_{i,t}, rewards R_t) assigned to each agent's SCM and which are designated as shared;
(ii) the structural assignments and exogenous noise variables that reproduce the Dec-POMDP transition kernel T, observation kernel O, and reward function R;
(iii) the causal graph (parent relations across time steps) for the multi-agent model;
and prove that the resulting MACM is equivalent to the Dec-POMDP in the sense that (a) the induced joint distribution over trajectories (S_{0:H}, A_{0:H}, O_{0:H}, R_{0:H}) coincides with that of the Dec-POMDP under any joint policy, and (b) interventional and counterfactual queries over alternative state–observation–action trajectories (e.g. "what reward would agent i have received had it taken action a'_{i,t} at time t, holding exogenous noises fixed") are well-defined and consistent across all agents that share the intervened-upon variables.
